live-draw: submit recorded work without waiting (chunks and walker idle points); walker profile split.

Symptom: in the Killzone 3 level (RSX pass 29) the FIFO walker / live-draw thread is the critical path:
162 ms of 237 ms per frame, 49 ms of it waiting for GPU fences in ~3.9 non-present flushes per frame. The
walker recorded a whole frame into one command list and handed it to the GPU only at a sync point (NV0039
scene copy write-back, main-surface write-back at a semaphore release, ring wrap, present), then waited for
all of it: the GPU idled while the walker recorded and the walker idled while the GPU ran.

Fix: no-wait submits (Close, ExecuteCommandLists, Signal, Reset on the same allocator) every
LD_SUBMIT_CHUNK draws (default 128, 0 = off) before a draw records anything, and when the walker goes
idle (caught up with put or parked on an unpatched JUMP; rsx_live_draw_submit_pending from the FIFO walk).
Sync points still wait before any guest memory or semaphore is written, so no guest-visible ordering
changes; rings, retired textures and readback slots are still recycled only after a fence wait. Not while
an occlusion query is open (patch 2025 counts a split query as two segments).

Per-draw PSO cost (the largest walker item in the r30a profile, 34 ms per frame): get_pso hashed the vertex
program twice per draw (the key and vp_hash are the same FNV-1a) and the fragment program once, byte by byte,
and called getenv() per draw for LD_GAMMA_LOG (every gamma texture unit) and LD_NO_ZALIAS_UNNORM_FIX. Now
the VP FNV is computed once, the VP/FP hashes and the FP size scan are memoized against a copy of the last
bytes (memcmp; exact, keys keep their values; LD_PSO_MEMO=0 turns it off), and both getenv calls are cached.

Diagnostics (off by default): RSX_PERF=1 with RSX_PERF_PROF=A,B prints "[perf-walk]" each second inside
that window (seconds since the first walk): draw time split into vertex fetch/copy, PSO lookup (new PSOs
and their build time), constants, texture binds (new decodes), clears, submit time, no-wait chunks, GPU
waits of readback flushes vs others, NV0039 and sync-point write-backs (count/time), the stores, and the
NV0039 guest copy, the texture content hash, and get_pso's parts (program sizes, texture-unit scan,
key, lookup; memo hits). RSX_PERF_SAMPLE=2 samples the walker thread itself inside the window, with the samples
outside the exe split by module ("[perf-samp-mod]"). LD_WB_UNSAFE_NOWAIT=1 is the host test's negative
control (write-backs read the readback buffers without waiting); never set it in a real run.

Test: tools/smoke/test_wb_batch.c (KZ3-shaped frame plus 150 extra draws, alternating patterns).

Rebased on ps3recomp v0.18.0 (SPU pass 108): context only (upstream v0.18.0 changed lines around the hunks; same change).

